title The wall that refuses only what this hand wrote
prompt the fleet baton -- BAKERY's lane, core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first
rota lap 4188, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes. Read the threshold, the Gall-Conway fusion, and skimmed the happy zone and growing-a-language. The cardinal question is the one that chose this lap: what smaller thing already works, and can this grow from it? readme_reach already works and already gates this class, so the wall reads ITS findings rather than parsing links again -- which is also what killed my first draft.
think Opened with fleet_round_open (already on the anointed order) and a cold roster pass. Three guards red, one root: construction/ITINERARY.md linked archive/REDS-a-label-is-not-its-target-rows-571.md where the shelf on disk is rows-570. Repaired first, reds before new work.
think That is REDS %524's fourth firing. Its own text names the lap and declines it: "a fifth pre-commit rule wants its own lap and inherits %515's reason to go slowly -- eight ships run that file, and a mistake in it is a fleet that cannot commit." That is my lane and my second duty both, so I took it.
think First draft parsed links itself, off the index, with its own path arithmetic. Swept 300 commits with it: nearly every commit reported broken > 0, which is implausible against a guard that holds the same class at zero. Debugged rather than believed. The findings were placeholder illustrations (date/YYYYMMDD/name, blessed by stamp-and-name) and pages the front door does not reach. Two derivations of one contract disagree -- %566's shape, one day old, caught before it shipped. Thrown away whole.
think Second draft takes no reading of its own. It runs tools/fixtures/r/readme_reach_scan.sh, takes that scan's own `living:` lines, and keeps the ones whose page `git diff --cached` says this commit staged. The gated class is a strict subset of the roster's by construction, so the wall can never refuse a commit the roster would accept.
think The safety property is the authorship split, not the speed. Eight ships share one card; a wall reading the whole tree would make one hand's broken link every hand's outage, which is precisely why %524 declined the lap. A break in an unstaged page is counted under `elsewhere` and refuses nothing.
obs The wall caught its own author inside the lap. Testing the red path I replanted the break with `sed 's|rows-571|rows-570|'`, which also rewrote INCENSE's line 164 where 571 was correct. `ours=2` named both. The derived spine warns about exactly that blind sweep; here it arrived as a measurement rather than as advice.
obs What it cannot see, said plainly rather than implied: three of %524's four firings are a link that was true when written and that a rebase falsified. git rebase runs no hook (%339) and the target existed at commit time. readme_reach at the roster owns that half. Naming the limit is what keeps the wall from being trusted past its reach.
obs %519's shape decision turned out already settled -- tools/fixtures/p/plant.sh landed today, rostered and witnessed, 12 of 175 controls sourcing it. The adoption sweep is a check-in its author left for Keaton, so I left it. My control authors every file it breaks, so the plant law has no claim to check there, and I said so in the header rather than importing it for appearance.
obs The card's booked "26 of 2,030 tools misfiled by letter room" measures 5 today, and the resolver recovers all five by index rather than losing them -- so the stated harm does not reproduce at the stated size. Not repaired this lap; recorded so the next hand starts from the number rather than from the booking.
obs Holding the tree still for the hot pass is what caught the last fault, and it was mine. The wall rides in a commit hook, and the reading it takes wants python3 -- so on a bench without it the wall would have refused EVERY commit, which is the fleet-that-cannot-commit outcome %524 declined this lap for, arriving through a different door. A missing capability is a bench fact rather than a page fact, so the wall now RESTS at verdict=rested_no_python and exits zero, and readme_reach at the roster keeps that reading where it belongs. Three more cases, proven by handing the wall a reach scan that answers nothing else. I stopped my own hot pass to fix it rather than shipping it and booking it.
